'''''A Game of Thrones''''' is an [[epic fantasy]] novel by American author [[George R. R. Martin]]. It was published in August 1996 as the first entry in his series ''[[A Song of Ice and Fire]]''. It was Martin's fourth novel and his return to writing prose fiction after a long period working in television. He had the initial idea in 1991 while writing science fiction; he wrote a hundred pages and submitted them to his agent, originally planning the novel as a trilogy.  

''A Game of Thrones'' is narrated in [[Third-person perspective|third person]], with each chapter alternating between eight protagonists who sometimes provide [[Unreliable narrator|unreliable accounts]]. In the [[Seven Kingdoms (A Song of Ice and Fire)|Seven Kingdoms]] of Westeros, [[House Stark]] and [[House Lannister]] influence the political fate of the continent. In Westeros' far north, an illegitimate son of House Stark joins a group maintaining a giant wall of ice to protect Westeros from [[Wildlings|raiders]] and [[Others (A Song of Ice and Fire)|a group of mythical enemies]]. Across the sea in Essos, the last surviving members of Westeros' deposed royal house, [[House Targaryen]], live in exile.

Following the novel's publication, several reviewers commended the novel's focus on political intrigue and historical influences. It won the 1997 [[Locus Award for Best Fantasy Novel]] and was nominated for several others, and a novella comprising the Targaryen chapters won the 1997 [[Hugo Award for Best Novella]]. The novel's first printing sold only a few thousand copies, but gained a small following through [[word of mouth]]. The HBO television adaptation ''[[Game of Thrones]]'' (2011–2019) reignited interest in the novel. It became a best-seller and the subject of academic and popular discourse. 

An epic fantasy novel, it has been widely compared with the work of [[J. R. R. Tolkien]] and characterized as subverting the genre's major tropes; it is sometimes described as historical or medieval fantasy. There are few direct historical analogues, but there are clear echoes of real history, like [[Hadrian's Wall]] inspiring the novel's giant wall of ice. Scholars have explored whether the novel authentically represents the Middle Ages and discuss how it responds to medieval literary conventions or themes, like [[chivalry]]. Gender, motherhood, and sexual violence are other frequently explored topics, and the authority of rulers or kings is sometimes discussed with reference to feudalism. In 2019, the [[BBC]] named it among the [[BBC's 100 Most Inspiring Novels|100 most inspiring novels]].

==Plot==
{{long plot|date=April 2026}}<!-- Per MOS:NOVELPLOT, a summary for a full-length novel should be between 400 and 700 words-->

===In the Seven Kingdoms===
After the death of Lord [[Jon Arryn]], King [[Robert Baratheon]] recruits his childhood friend [[Eddard Stark|Eddard "Ned" Stark]], the lord of the northern castle of [[Winterfell]], to replace Arryn as Hand of the King and betroths his son [[Joffrey Baratheon|Joffrey]] to Ned's daughter [[Sansa Stark|Sansa]]. Shortly thereafter, Ned's son [[Bran Stark|Bran]] discovers Robert's wife, Queen [[Cersei Lannister]], having sex with her twin brother, [[Jaime Lannister|Jaime]]. Jaime throws Bran from a tower, leaving Bran comatose and paralyzed. 

Ned brings his daughters Sansa and [[Arya Stark|Arya]] to the capital city, [[King's Landing (A Song of Ice and Fire)|King's Landing]], and finds that Robert is an ineffective king whose only interests are hunting, drinking, and womanizing.

At Winterfell, an assassin fails to kill Bran while he is unconscious, and Ned's wife, [[Catelyn Stark|Catelyn]], travels to King's Landing to bring word to Ned. Catelyn's childhood friend, [[Petyr Baelish|Petyr "Littlefinger" Baelish]], implicates [[Tyrion Lannister]], the [[dwarfism|dwarf]] brother of Cersei and Jaime, in the assassination attempt. On the road home, Catelyn encounters Tyrion and arrests him for the attempt on Bran's life. In retaliation for Tyrion's abduction, his father, Lord [[Tywin Lannister]], sends soldiers to raid [[World of A Song of Ice and Fire#The Riverlands|the Riverlands]], Catelyn's home region. Tyrion regains his freedom by demanding a trial and recruiting a mercenary named [[Bronn (character)|Bronn]] to defend him in [[trial by combat]].

Ned investigates Jon Arryn's death and discovers that the children thought to be Robert's heirs are in fact Cersei's children by Jaime; he infers that Jon Arryn was killed to conceal this information. Before Ned can act, Cersei arranges Robert's death and installs Joffrey on the throne. Ned enlists Littlefinger's help to take Cersei and Joffrey into custody. Littlefinger betrays him and Ned is arrested. Arya escapes, but Cersei and Joffrey take Sansa as a hostage.

Ned's eldest son, [[Robb Stark|Robb]], marches his army south in response to his father's arrest. To secure a strategically necessary bridge crossing, Catelyn negotiates a [[marital alliance]] between Robb and the notoriously unreliable House Frey. Robb defeats a Lannister army in the Riverlands, capturing Jaime. Tywin sends Tyrion back to King's Landing to act as Joffrey's Hand of the King. Joffrey has Ned executed and Robb's followers declare independence from the Seven Kingdoms, proclaiming Robb the "King in the North".

===On the Wall===
The [[prologue]] of the novel introduces [[the Wall (A Song of Ice and Fire)|the Wall]]: a huge magical wall of ice at the northern border of the Seven Kingdoms. The Wall is defended by the [[Night's Watch]], a great order of warriors who serve for life to defend the realm from the [[White Walker|Others]], an ancient and hostile inhuman race, and human "[[wildlings]]" who live north of the Wall.

[[Jon Snow (character)|Jon Snow]], Ned's [[Legitimacy (family law)|bastard son]], is inspired by [[Benjen Stark|his uncle Benjen]] to join the Night's Watch. He becomes disillusioned when he discovers that its recruits are mostly [[penal colony|convicts]]. Jon unites his fellow recruits against their harsh instructor and protects the cowardly but good-natured and intelligent [[Samwell Tarly]]. Jon is appointed steward to the leader of the Watch, Lord Commander [[Jeor Mormont]]. Benjen fails to return from an expedition north of the Wall; when the bodies of two men from his party are recovered, they re-animate as undead [[wight]]s before being dispatched by Jon.

When word of Ned's execution reaches Jon, he attempts to join Robb against the Lannisters, but is persuaded to remain loyal to the Watch. Mormont declares his intention to march north to find Benjen, and to investigate rumors of a "King-beyond-the-Wall" uniting the wildlings.

===Across the Narrow Sea===
Across the eastern sea, in Essos, live the exiled children of the dead and deposed Mad King [[Aerys Targaryen|Aerys]] of the Seven Kingdoms: [[Viserys Targaryen|Viserys]] and [[Daenerys Targaryen]].

Viserys betroths Daenerys to [[Khal Drogo]], a warlord of the [[Dothraki]] people, in exchange for the use of Drogo's army to reclaim the throne. Daenerys receives three petrified dragon eggs as a wedding present from a wealthy merchant. [[Jorah Mormont]], a knight exiled from Westeros and Jeor's son, joins Viserys as an adviser. Initially terrified of her new husband and his people, Daenerys eventually embraces her role as Drogo's queen. Drogo, however, shows little interest in conquering Westeros. When Viserys publicly threatens Daenerys and her unborn child, Drogo executes him by pouring molten gold on his head.

An assassin attempts to poison Daenerys, convincing Drogo to conquer Westeros. While sacking villages to fund the invasion, Drogo is badly wounded, and Daenerys commands the captive [[folk healer]] Mirri Maz Duur to save him. The healer, angered by the Dothraki raids against her people, sacrifices Daenerys's unborn child to power the spell to save Drogo's life, which restores Drogo's physical health but leaves him in a [[persistent vegetative state]].

With Drogo incapacitated, much of his army disperses. Daenerys smothers Drogo with a pillow and has Mirri tied to Drogo's funeral pyre. She places her three dragon eggs on the pyre and enters it herself. When the fire burns out, she emerges unharmed, with three newly hatched dragons.

==Background==

===Author===
[[File:GRRM_Ljubljana_(cropped).jpg|thumb|Martin at a book signing in [[Ljubljana]], Slovenia (2011)]]
[[George R. R. Martin]] was born and raised in [[Bayonne, New Jersey|Bayonne]], New Jersey.{{Sfn|Berwick|2012}} His family was poor,{{Sfn|Martin|2014}} lived on a government [[Public housing|housing project]], and did not own a car.{{Sfn|Levy|1996}} He started writing in childhood and sold horror stories to other children for a nickel.{{Sfn|Martin|2014}}{{Sfn|Macnab|2014}} Martin's passion for comic books led to his interest in science fiction and fantasy and, as a teenager, he wrote [[superhero fiction]] for [[Fan magazine|fan magazines]].{{Sfn|Flood|2018}} His first published work was a science-fiction story for ''[[Galaxy Science Fiction]]'' in 1971.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=1–2}}{{Sfn|Martin|2014}} At [[Northwestern University]], Martin studied journalism,{{Sfn|Flood|2018}} and he was a [[conscientious objector]] to the [[Vietnam War]]{{Sfn|St. James|2017}}—he performed [[Alternative civilian service|alternative service]] instead.{{Sfn|Flood|2018}}  

Martin's first two published novels, [[Dying of the Light (Martin novel)|''Dying of the Light'']] (1977) and ''[[Fevre Dream]]'' (1982), performed well,{{Sfn|Flood|2018}}{{Sfn|Martin|2014}} and by 1983 he had accrued 3 [[Hugo Award]]s.{{Sfn|Huddleston Jr.|2019}} Martin's publisher provided a large [[Advance against royalties|advance payment]] for his third novel, ''[[The Armageddon Rag]]'' (1983).{{Sfn|Huddleston Jr.|2019}} It was a commercial disaster, temporarily halting his literary career.{{Sfn|Shindler|2005}} A fan of ''The Armageddon Rag'' hired Martin as a writer on a revival of [[The Twilight Zone (1985 TV series)|''The Twilight Zone'']] (1985–1989);{{Sfn|Huddleston Jr.|2019}} he later worked on the [[CBS]] series [[Beauty and the Beast (1987 TV series)|''Beauty and the Beast'']] (1987–1990) and [[The Outer Limits (1995 TV series)|''The Outer Limits'']] (1995–1997).{{Sfn|Flood|2018}}{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=2}} This work paid Martin well,{{Sfn|Levy|1996}} but he was frustrated by the limitations of television budgets and the early cancellation of projects.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=1–2}}

===Writing and publication===
Martin was writing a [[science fiction]] novel titled ''Avalon'' in summer 1991,{{Sfn|Martin|2014}} and had wanted to write an epic fantasy novel since reading the work of English writer [[J. R. R. Tolkien]].{{Sfn|Martin|2011}} He had an idea for the first chapter – a young boy seeing a beheading and the discovery of [[List of Game of Thrones characters#Direwolves|direwolf]] pups in "summer snows"{{Sfn|Martin|2014}}{{Sfn|Snider|2025}} – and wrote it in a few days.{{Sfn|Martin|2011}} Over that summer, he sketched a map, wrote a hundred pages, then sent the pages – alongside a series outline – to his [[literary agent]]. He expected he was writing a trilogy because they were the standard for the fantasy genre, and [[Worldbuilding|built the world]] as he wrote the story.{{Sfn|Martin|2014}}{{Efn|Martin classifies writers' approaches into two types—"architects" meticulously plan ahead and "gardeners" have a general sense of the end result but no rigid outline; he considers himself a "gardener".{{Sfn|Martin NPR|2019}}}} Martin pointed to historical fiction as an important influence, and described French writer [[Maurice Druon]]'s seven-book historical series ''[[The Accursed Kings]]'' as "the original game of thrones".{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=108}} He describes epic fantasy and historical fiction as siblings.{{Sfn|Cornwell|2012}}{{Efn|For many readers, Martin's work has become the primary reference for the Middle Ages.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=17–18}} Literature scholar Helen Young argues this has negatively impacted discussions about the series' authentic representation.{{Sfn|Young|2014|pp=740–742}}}}

''A Game of Thrones'' was published in August 1996 by [[Bantam Books]] (USA) and [[HarperCollins|HarperCollins Voyager]] (UK).{{Sfn|Miller|2012|p=198}} The cover bore a recommendation from ''[[The Wheel of Time]]'' author [[Robert Jordan]];{{Sfn|Power|2021}} the UK edition positioned him as an heir to Tolkien.{{Sfn|Jeffery|1997|p=19}} Several thousand copies were printed in the United States; only 1500 were produced for the United Kingdom.{{Sfn|Schubart|Gjelsvik|2016|p=3}} Bantam's edition was printed several months earlier so that copies could be disseminated at the June 1996 [[American Booksellers Association]] convention. Further copies were distributed at the July [[Westercon]].{{Sfn|Miller|2012|p=198}}

==Style==

===Narration===
''A Game of Thrones'' is narrated in the [[Third-person perspective|third person]] from a character's [[Narration#Omniscient or limited|limited]] perspective, alternating character with each chapter.{{Sfn|Padol|1997|p=23}} Martin primarily uses noble-born perspective characters,{{Sfn|Wells-Lassagne|2016|pp=41, 52–53}} which Shannon Wells-Lassagne says keeps the reader's interest and loyalty with the aristocratic houses.{{Sfn|Wells-Lassagne|2016|p=52}} A non-noble member of the Night's Watch, named Will, focalizes the novel's prologue.{{Sfn|Wells-Lassagne|2016|p=52}}  

There are three main plot lines. Daenerys Targaryen provides the sole viewpoint on her exile.{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|p=63}} Concurrently, the politics of the Seven Kingdoms mainly unfolds through the perspectives of House Stark members—Ned, Catelyn, Sansa, Arya, and Bran—dispersed across Westeros, with Tyrion Lannister also offering a viewpoint.{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|p=63}} Finally, Jon Snow's service to the Night's Watch on the far northern Wall forms the third plot line.{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|p=63}}   

Martin's viewpoint characters often provide [[Unreliable narrator|unreliable accounts]];{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|pp=60–61}} Brian Pavlac describes the viewpoints as "sources" sometimes at odds with each other.{{Sfn|Pavlac Introduction|p=3}} Some narrators actively suppress their thoughts to conceal information from the reader.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=28–29}} A notable example is the true parentage of Ned Stark's supposed illegitimate son, Jon Snow.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=28–29}} Ned's recurring memory of his dying sister Lyanna's plea—"Promise me, Ned"—provides the reader with more information about the circumstances of her death as the novel progresses.{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|pp=61–62}}

===Genre===
Martin described the series as [[epic fantasy]] "inspired by and grounded in history",{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=121}}  and some scholars concur.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=1}}{{Sfn|Finn|2020|p=29}}{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=16}} Others apply labels like modern fantasy,{{Sfn|Young|2019|p=1}}{{Sfn|Blaszkiewicz|2014|p=9|ref={{sfnref|Blaszkiewicz in Blaszkiewicz}}}} romance fantasy,{{Sfn|Blaszkiewicz|2014a|p=115}}{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=183}} medieval or neomedievalist fantasy,{{Sfn|Rohr|Benz|2020|p=xxxiii}}{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=60}}{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=20}} historical fantasy, and fantastical history.{{Sfn|Uckelman|Murphy|Percer|2017|p=248}} Literary scholar Shiloh Carroll notes a broad range of influences for Martin's series,{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=182}} and writes that Martin's attempts to subvert or avoid medievalist literary conventions resulted in thematic overlap with Victorian medieval romance.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=183}}

Commentators often compare Martin's work and universe with those of [[J. R. R. Tolkien|Tolkien]].{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=107}}{{Sfn|Staggs|2012|p=149}} Martin himself has compared his work to Tolkien's and its imitators,{{Sfn|Rohr|Benz|2020|p=xxxiv}} although he characterized Tolkien's approach as too simplified.{{Sfn|Walton|2019|p=100}} As with ''The Lord of the Rings'', ''A Game of Thrones'' was initially planned as the first in a trilogy.{{Sfn|Pavlac Introduction|p=3}} Carroll says both writers are indebted to medieval sources, but Tolkien drew from medieval legend where Martin draws from history.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=3}} Literary critic Priscilla Walton describes Martin's work as "more [[Byzantine Empire|Byzantine]] (and less [[Christian worldview|Christian]])".{{Sfn|Walton|2019|p=100}} However, Joseph Rex Young argues that positioning Martin's work against Tolkien's neglects to consider their respective professional backgrounds.{{Efn|Young describes ''The Lord of the Rings'' as "a deeply personal experiment in linguistics, medievalism and moral philosophy" and emphasises Tolkien's lifelong academic career. Concerning Martin, Young describes his background as a writer of science fiction and superhero stories, "and as such he fits the profile of a typical genre fantasist".{{Sfn|Young|2019|pp=2–4}}}}

The series has been widely celebrated for [[Subversion|subverting]] fantasy tropes;{{Sfn|Mitchell|2018|p=230}} Ned Stark's death is often regarded as the moment ''A Game of Thrones'' "became a distinctive, original contribution" to the genre.{{Sfn|Young|2019|p=9}}{{Efn|Of Stark's death, Young notes that "Eddard dies, almost literally, on-stage, in front of a crowd, in a manner carefully arranged by characters and author alike for maximum dramatic effect."{{Sfn|Young|2019|p=9}}}} Ned Vizzini suggests that works of fantasy preceding Martin focused on characters of low birth and station,{{Sfn|Vizzini|2012|p=211}} and argues that Martin elevated fantasy by "writing books that are too bloody, unexpected, and relentlessly story-driven to be ignored".{{Sfn|Vizzini|2012|pp=216–217}} However, Joseph Rex Young argues that Martin does not "[overturn] the fundamental rules of fantasy" but instead "[follows] them to great effect".{{Sfn|Young|2019|p=4}} He calls it a "shrewdly assembled example of the modern fantasy form",{{Sfn|Young|2019|pp=8}} contending—for example—that various plot lines align with [[Rhetorics of Fantasy|Mendlesohnian fantasy categorizations]]—for example, Daenerys and Bran's journeys as [[portal-quest fantasy]].{{Sfn|Young|2019|pp=6, 104–105; 90}}{{Efn|In portal-quest fantasy, characters enter environments they do not understand and acquire information from guide characters, with the reader learning alongside the character and prevented from learning what they do not know.{{Sfn|Young|2019|p=72}} For example: Bran is advised by the Three-Eyed Crow, whose dialogue is not represented by speech marks and are thus "matters of narrative truth".{{Sfn|Young|2019|p=104}}}} 

In ''A Game of Thrones'', magic belongs to a mythological or lost past.{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|p=54}}{{Sfn|Symons|2017|p=94}} Young states this theme of a lost or diminished world is "pervasive" in modern fantasy.{{Sfn|Young|2019|p=6}} Symons specifically identifies the dragon eggs as representing a lost past, noting that their birth is remarkable because it establishes ''A Game of Thrones'' as supernatural fantasy at the novel's conclusion.{{Sfn|Symons|2017|p=94}} In Westeros, characters generally downplay magic, relegating it to [[superstition]],{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|pp=52–53}} as seen in omens,{{Efn|Gary Westfahl, for instance, highlights: "Lord Eddard Stark agrees to spare a litter of dire-wolf pups when his bastard son, Jon Snow, points out that they correspond in their number and genders to his own children."{{Sfn|Westfahl|2012|p=56}}}} cursed or haunted places, [[Apotropaic magic|apotropaic runes]], and resurrection. Excluding the novel's prologue and events involving Daenerys and the ''maegi'', Young counts three explicitly supernatural events.{{Sfn|Young|2019|pp=138–139}}

==Reception==
Upon release, ''A Game of Thrones'' was widely praised.{{Sfn|Symons|2017|p=95}} Don D'Ammassa suggested it as "the major fantasy publishing event of 1996".{{Sfn|D'Ammassa|1996}} In ''[[The Year's Best Science Fiction]]'' (1997), [[Gardner Dozois|Gardner R. Dozois]] described the novel as "the year's Big Fantasy Novel, reviewed everywhere", and a favorite for the World Fantasy Award.{{Sfn|Dozois|1997|p=xxxii}} The first printing sold a few thousand copies.{{Sfn|Lowder Introduction|2012|p=xiii|ref={{sfnref|Lowder in Lowder}}}} While not an immediate commercial hit, some independent booksellers championed the novel and it gained a small following through [[word of mouth]].{{Sfn|Miller|2011}} It won the [[Locus Award for Best Fantasy Novel]] and the 1997 [[Premio Ignotus]] for best foreign novel.{{Sfn|Lowder Introduction|2012|p=xiv|ref={{sfnref|Lowder in Lowder}}}}

A novella titled '''''Blood of the Dragon''''', comprising Daenerys' chapters and published in ''[[Asimov's Science Fiction]]'', won the 1997 [[Hugo Award for Best Novella]].{{Sfn|Symons|2017|p=95}}

Contemporary reviews celebrated Martin writing prose fiction again after a long absence.{{Sfnm|1a1=Jeffery|1y=1997|1p=19|2a1=Perry|2y=1996|3a1=Shindler|3y=1996|3p=18|4a1=Publishers Weekly|4y=1996}} Several observed that readers would eagerly await a sequel.{{Sfn|D'Ammassa|1996|p=76}}{{Sfn|Nathan|1996}}{{Sfn|Mazzacco|1996}} Jeff Watkins of the ''[[Albuquerque Journal]]'' said that "[a]fter so many pages, a reader wants to know how the thing comes out".{{Sfn|Watkins|1996}} Two reviews said the ending provided little narrative resolution,{{Sfn|Kershaw|1996}}{{Sfn|Kirkus|1996}} and it was framed as the first instalment of a trilogy.{{Sfn|Shindler|1996|p=18}}{{Sfn|Watkins|1996}}{{Sfn|Padol|1997|p=23}} [[Vector (journal)|''Vector'']]{{'s}} Steve Jeffery said that [[HarperCollins]]' marketing had done Martin's novel a disservice by comparing it to ''The Lord of the Rings'' and described ''AGOT'' as "widescreen epic fantasy, well delivered and competently told".{{Sfn|Jeffery|1997|p=19}} ''[[The New York Review of Science Fiction]]''{{'s}} Lisa Padol said it "aspires to be a page-turner" more than epic fantasy.{{Sfn|Padol|1997|p=23}}   

Several reviews, including by [[Phyllis Eisenstein]] for the ''[[Chicago Sun-Times]]'', said Martin's execution elevated a conventional fantasy set-up;{{Sfnm|1a1=Eisenstein|1y=1996|2a1=Kershaw|2y=1996|3a1=Miller|3y=1996|3pp=20–21|4a1=Shindler|4y=1996|4p=18}} [[Dave Gross]] writes that he "makes vital figures of what seems to be stock characters".{{Sfn|Gross|1996|p=59}} Dorman Shindler of ''[[The Des Moines Register]]'' said Martin imbued the Stark children with as many weaknesses as their antagonists.{{Sfn|Shindler|1996|p=18}} An anonymous 1999 review by ''[[The Guardian]]'' described the characters as "so venomous they could eat the [[House of Borgia|Borgias]]".{{Sfn|The Guardian|1999}} ''[[The Washington Post]]''{{'s}} John H. Riskind criticized them as one dimensional.{{Sfn|Riskind|1996}} In [[Interzone (magazine)|''Interzone'']], [[Gwyneth Jones (novelist)|Gwyneth Jones]] criticized the major female characters as "fools and rotters" except Arya Stark; she said Daenerys has "no characteristics except a will of iron".{{Sfn|Jones|1996|p=59}}  

Reviewers frequently praised the intrigue and emphasis on politics.{{Sfnm|1a1=Daemon|1y=1996|1p=30|2a1=Mazzacco|2y=1996|2p=119|3a1=Nathan|3y=1996|4a1=Scunthorpe Telegraph|4y=1998|4p=14|5a1=The Guardian|5y=1999|5p=278}} The [[Associated Press]]' review commended the narrative structure;{{Sfn|Nathan|1996}} ''[[Kirkus Reviews]]'' praised the characters and complex plot "flawlessly articulated against a backdrop of real depth and texture".{{Sfn|Kirkus|1996}} Anticipating future instalments, ''[[Booklist]]'' said the novel was likely to reward rereading, but described the large cast as a "daunting" burden of the fantasy genre.{{Sfn|Green|1996}} Several reviewers mentioned Martin's influence from historical narratives; Jones compared the novel to ''[[Macbeth]]'' and paralleled Ned Stark's intransigence with Scottish [[Reformed Christianity|Calvinists]].{{Sfn|Jones|1996|p=59}} Padol identified the ice wall with [[Hadrian's Wall]] and compared the [[dothraki]] to the [[Mongols]].{{Sfn|Padol|1997|p=23}} In [[Locus (magazine)|''Locus'']], Shira Daemon said the novel felt closer to historical fiction than fantasy, with supernatural threats that would not pay off until future entries.{{Sfn|Daemon|1996|p=57}} A second review the following month by Faren Millar called it "a medievalesque fantistorical novel".{{Sfn|Miller|1996|p=20}}

The success of the HBO adaptation ''[[Game of Thrones]]'' (2011–2019) reignited interest in the novel, making it a best seller and the subject of popular and academic interest.{{Sfn|Symons|2017|p=95}} ''[[BBC|BBC Arts]]'' named ''A Game of Thrones'' among the [[BBC list of 100 'most inspiring' novels|100 most influential novels]] in 2019.{{Sfn|BBC|2019}} The novel was on the [[New York Times Bestseller List|''New York Times'' Bestseller List]] in January 2011,{{Sfn|NYT 1|2011}} and was the top of the list in July 2011.{{Sfn|NYT 2|2011}}

