}}
Scholars have explored Martin's depiction of the [[Middle Ages]] and ideals associated with the period.{{Efn|David Symons notes that Martin's work has featured in medievalist university classes.{{Sfn|Symons|2017|p=94}} Bartlomiej Blaszkiewicz narrows it to the High Middle Ages.{{Sfn|Blaszkiewicz|2014a|p=115}}}} [[Carolyne Larrington]] and Joanna Kakot identify the influence of medieval Europe.{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|p=55}}{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=16}} KellyAnn Fitzpatrick describes ''A Song of Ice and Fire'' as neomedieval fantasy, indebted to "medieval history, medieval myth, and later medievalist and neomedieval interpretations".{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=103}} Carol Jamison describes the setting as detailed and intricate "a pseudo-medieval society".{{Sfn|Jamison|2018|p=182}}  Priscila L. Walton says the series' likeness to the Middle Ages is superficial, but ''A Game of Thrones''' [[Feudalism|feudalist society]] bears the closest similarity of any volume in the series.{{Sfn|Walton|2019|p=101}}{{Efn|Some fans the series believe that the series is authentically medieval.{{Sfn|Benz|Rohr|2020|p=xxix}}{{Sfn|Young|2014|pp=741–742}} Carroll argues it is unfeasible to create an authentic medieval portrayal and that Martin sought to create an impression of reality.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=14, 17–18}}}}

Some writers explore the novel's treatment of medieval [[Chivalry|chivalric]] conventions. Shiloh Carroll describes the novel as subverting the figure of the [[knight-errant]] and idea of honorable nobility.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=38}} Carolyne Larrington notes that some knights publicly keep with "the tenets of chivalry": [[Jaime Lannister|Jaime]] does not kill Ned during their duel, and is angry at a soldier who injures Ned in the middle of it.{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=129}} According to Carroll, Sansa Stark is presented as an idealist, and Ned Stark as the likely [[Chivalric romance|romantic hero]], then corrects Sansa's notions of a just aristocracy.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=38}} Blaszkiewicz says that male sexuality is frequently depicted as "disruptive, if not explicitly violent" in chivalric romance;{{Sfn|Blaszkiewicz|2014a|p=86}} Alyssa Rosenberg notes that King Robert [[Domestic violence|abuses]] his queen and [[Marital rape|commits marital rape]], contravening chivalric ideals.{{Sfn|Rosenberg|2012|p=21}} In the novel, romantic or idealistic characters die or have their faiths broken, foregrounding the novel's inspirations but acknowledging the limited appeal of romantic ideals to modern audiences.{{Sfn|Fowler|2014|pp=71–72}} Medievalist Steven Muhlberger says the primary chivalric institutions—chiefly the Night's Watch and the Kingsguard—represent the erosion of chivalric standards.{{Sfn|Muhlberger|2017|pp=52–53}} The Night's Watch dedicate their lives to protecting the realm, but are composed of condemned criminals. The Kingsguard might resemble "royally-sponsored orders of chivalry from the Middle Ages", but are selected for political reasons and not for skill or leadership.{{Sfn|Muhlberger|2017|pp=52–53}}  

Martin generally avoids direct historical analogy,{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=110}} but there are clear allusions.{{Sfn|Jamison|2018|p=182}} Westeros' history of invasion may represent the [[Roman conquest of Britain|Roman]], [[Anglo-Saxon settlement of Britain|Anglo-Saxon]], and [[Norman Conquest|Norman conquests of England]].{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=110–111}} Medieval scholar Kavita Mudan Finn notes that [[Cersei Lannister|Cersei]]'s introduction underscores her loyalty to House Lannister over her husband the king, noting parallels with [[Edward IV]]'s marriage to [[Elizabeth Woodville]], which brought her ambitious family to court and ultimately culminated in civil war.{{Sfn|Finn|2017|pp=19–20}} Several scholars note resemblance between House Stark / [[House of York]] and House Lannister / [[House of Lancaster]].{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|pp=107–108}}{{Sfn|Schubart|Gjelsvik|2016|p=6}}{{Sfn|Pavlac Introduction|p=8}} Larrington describes the presumed deaths of two young Targaryen heirs in the novel's pre-history as a motif recalling the 15th-century [[Princes in the Tower]];{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=17}} she compares [[Petyr Baelish]] to [[Geoffrey Chaucer]]—citing his low birth and penchant for [[Parvenu|political climbing]]{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|pp=129–130}}—and [[Khal Drogo]] to a fictional version of [[Attila]].{{Sfnm|1a1=Larrington|1y=2019|1p=192|2a1=Young|2y=2019|2p=56}} Westeros' primary religious institution, the Faith of the Seven, may resemble the medieval [[Catholic Church]], although less powerful.{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=133}}{{Efn|Larrington writes: "Although Ned remarks to Catelyn, "it’s your religion
which has all the rules," it's quite hard to distinguish between the different mores produced by class, gender or ethnic differences and those derived from the Faith’s religious teachings."{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=134}}}} Several critics compare the novel's Wall to [[Hadrian's Wall]],{{Sfnm|1a1=Carroll|1y=2017|1p=75|2a1=de Ruiter|2y=2017|2pp=85–87|3a1=Young|3y=2018|3p=68}}{{Efn|Martin has said he had the idea of the Wall in the early 1980s while visiting a friend in England.{{Sfn|Martin|2014}}}} while others have noted a similarity to the [[Great Wall of China]].{{Sfn|Young|2018|p=68}} Kakot compares the Dothraki culture to nomadic North African tribes,{{Sfn|Kokot|2014|p=55}} and Larrington highlights additional influence from central Asian cultures.{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=16}}

===Gender and sex===
[[File:Louis vii and alienor.jpg|thumb|alt=Manuscript illumination|Medieval illumination of the wedding between [[Eleanor of Aquitaine]] and [[Louis VII of France]]. They were betrothed when she was nine and he was fifteen as part of a political alliance. Her life formed part of the historical background for the novel's inspiration.{{Sfn|West|2019}}]]
Several female characters are entered into marriage to cement alliances or facilitate a transfer of wealth.{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=122}} Viserys trades his sister Daenerys for military support from Khal Drogo.{{Sfnm|1a1=Borowska-Szerszun|1y=2020|1p=64|2a1=Larsson|2y=2016|2p=23|3a1=Walton|3y=2019|3p=108|4a1=Larrington|4y=2020|4p=xi}} Borowska-Szerszun says that Daenerys' narrative—marriage and loss of freedom—traditionally conclude women's stories in [[Fairy tale|fairy tales]], but affords Daenerys with power and social status.{{Sfn|Borowska-Szerszun|2020|p=64}} Larrington says Daenerys' growing influence over Drogo weakens his standing as a leader,{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=34}} and her decisions cause the end of Drogo's rule and the birth of her dragons.{{Sfn|Larrington|2019|p=34}} Before the events of ''A Game of Thrones'', Cersei Lannister and Catelyn Stark are married, to Robert and Ned respectively, to establish political alliances.{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=122}}{{Sfn|Larrington|2020|p=x}} Cersei and Catelyn's children—Joffrey and Sansa—are betrothed to ensure the north's loyalty to the crown.{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=122}} This betrothal is ultimately abusive and she is held hostage and used as a pawn.{{Sfn|Larsson|2016|p=31}}{{Efn|Carroll describes Cersei as a [[Foil (narrative)|narrative foil]] to Sansa: the "archetypal princess" and the "bitter and power-hungry queen".{{Sfn|Carroll|2014|p=250}}}} Whether the series' female characters constitute [[feminism|feminist]] portrayals is a contested topic.{{Sfn|Schubart|Gjelsvik|2016|p=2}}{{Sfn|Benz|Rohr|2020|p=xliii}}

Scholars and fans often discuss the series' frequent depiction of rape, which is sometimes described by Martin and fans as historically accurate.{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|pp=10, 18}} Medieval women were subject to [[marital rape]].{{Sfn|Alesi|2017|p=166}} Mariah Larsson states that Drogo knows only one word in Daenerys' language, "no", and that he uses this word to "ensure his wife's consent" before [[consummation|consummating]] the marriage.{{Sfn|Larsson|2016|p=23}} According to Carroll, Daenerys' age and circumstances "problematize" the consent and he says Drogo "rapes her every night on the way to Vaes Dothrak", ultimately "[falling] in love with her attacker".{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=93}}{{Efn|Carroll also writes: "The difficulty with rape in ''A Song of Ice and Fire'' is that commentators have trouble differentiating between authorial endorsement and portrayal. Martin's narrative voice, hidden as it is behind the third-person viewpoint with which he writes the series, clearly does not approve of rape or violence in general."{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=92}}}} Daenerys tries to prevent or mitigate sexual violence. She saves a group of women from rape by her husband's warriors by claiming them as handmaidens.{{Sfn|Walton|2019|p=108}} Although Daenerys directly intervenes to prevent the rape of another woman, the warriors return later to [[gang rape]] her;{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=92}} Carroll describes this as "an expression of ownership and power over a woman [and] a vengeance against Daenerys for denying the men's claim to Eroeh earlier".{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=92}} Cersei is subject to sexual violence by Robert, who blames it on alcohol.{{Sfn|Spector|2012|p=185}}

Some scholars discuss the novel's representation of motherhood. Marta Eidsvåg contrasts Cersei's role as a mother against her ordering the assassination of Robert's illegitimate children.{{Sfn|Eidsvåg|2016|pp=153–154, 165}} Catelyn Stark is depicted as devoted to her children, but she acts hatefully towards Ned's illegitimate son Jon.{{Sfn|Schubart|Gjelsvik|2016|p=6}} Catelyn's role as a viewpoint character is unusual because mothers are not typically depicted in fantasy.{{Sfn|Schubart|Gjelsvik|2016|p=6}} Robert does not think Daenerys herself presents a direct threat to his rule, but he is deeply alarmed by the news of her marriage and the prospect of offspring.{{Sfn|Liedl|2017|p=125}} Carroll notes symbolism that frames Daenerys as the dragons' mother: "the eggs begin to hatch, lactating in 'streams'; when the fire dies [...] two of the dragons are nursing at her breasts".{{Sfn|Carroll|2018|p=78}}

Several writers have explored Daenerys' encounter with the ''maegi'' Mirri Maz Duur''.'' Sheilagh O'Brien describes the ''maegi'' as a conventional representation of witches, symbolizing anxieties over female power, "monstrous births, and the influence of an evil elderly woman over a younger woman often encountered in early modern witch narratives".{{Sfn|O'Brien|2020|p=209}} Anne Gjelsvik writes that Mirri Maz Duur represents Daenerys' denial of her role in oppression. She attempts to save Mirri Maz Duur from [[gang rape]] but fails. The ''maegi'' takes vengeance by causing Daenerys' child to be [[Stillbirth|stillborn]]. Consequently, Daenerys takes the ''maegi'' into Drogo's funeral pyre and symbolically consumes the witch's magic.{{Sfn|Gjelsvik|2016|pp=64, 118–119}}

===Power and rulers===
Kingship and royal power is frequently explored by scholars. Blaszkiewicz says the tyrannical Aerys led the country to civil war by destroying the [[social contract]] between king and community. Robert is an improvement over Aerys' tyranny but is "equally oblivious to the notion of royal dignity involving a [king's] social duty".{{Sfn|Blaszkiewicz|2014a|pp=118–119}} Blaszkiewicz argues his decadent lifestyle and appearance demonstrate his inability to properly execute the social role of a king.{{Sfn|Blaszkiewicz|2014a|p=119}} Hudson says that Cersei dismisses Robert's authority by derisively overruling his writ declaring Ned Stark the regent, taking the role herself.{{Sfn|Hudson|2020|p=9}} Walton says Ned's function as judge and executioner makes his role as the north's [[Overlord|feudal lord]] effectively equivalent to that of a king—a role his son inherits when Ned goes to King's Landing.{{Sfn|Walton|2019|p=103}}

Pavlac says that the ''game of thrones'' refers to attempts to gain control over the Seven Kingdoms of Westeros.{{Sfn|Pavlac Introduction|p=9}} Cersei Lannister refers to ''the game of thrones'' in the novel, telling Ned that participation means [[victory or death]].{{Sfn|Hudson|2020|p=10}} King Robert's death, and the resultant disputed succession, drives it—Robert's brothers claim the throne because Robert's heirs are illegitimate.{{Sfn|Fitzpatrick|2019|p=107}}

==Adaptations==
{{main|Game of Thrones (franchise)}}
A novella titled '''''Blood of the Dragon''''', comprising [[Daenerys Targaryen]]'s chapters from ''A Game of Thrones'' and published in ''[[Asimov's Science Fiction]]'', was released in July 1996.{{Sfn|Symons|2017|p=95}}

''A Song of Ice and Fire'' is the basis for the [[HBO]] television series ''[[Game of Thrones]]'' (2011–2019). The first season was broadly faithful to ''A Game of Thrones'', but later the series diverged more from the novels.{{Sfn|Bruner|2019}} A major change for the series was adding several years to the novel's timeline, increasing several characters' ages: Sansa (from eleven to thirteen), Arya (from nine to eleven), Bran (from seven to ten),{{Sfn|Larsson|2016|pp=22–23}} and Daenerys (thirteen to fifteen).{{Sfn|Schubart|Gjelsvik|2016|p=8}}   

''A Game of Thrones'' was adapted as [[A Game of Thrones (comics)|a graphic novel of the same name]] by author [[Daniel Abraham (author)|Daniel Abraham]],{{Sfn|Abraham|2012|p=29}} with art by Tommy Patterson.{{Sfn|Young|2018|p=60}} Anne Groell, who edited the original novel, requested that Abraham outline his proposed approach to the adaptation.{{Sfn|Abraham|2012|p=29}} Abraham described several problems in adapting the work: predicting what was vital to preserve in an unfinished series; how to visualize elements that already existed in the popular imagination; and [[PROTECT Act of 2003|a US child abuse law]] precluding an illustration of young Daenerys in a sexual context.{{Sfn|Abraham|2012|p=29}}

''[[A Game of Thrones: Genesis]]'' (2011) draws its name from the novel. A [[real-time strategy]] game, it was the first video game title to use the ''Game of Thrones'' license. It does not depict the events from the novel but uses setting elements spanning the millennium preceding it.{{Sfn|Schröter|2016|p=89}}

